+
+### ADR-143, Amendment 8 (26 November 2026) — a drawn item's source document is told only to a reader who may read it when it is sent, and a drawn item needs a source note — Proposed for the owner's approval
+
+Status: **Proposed**, for the owner's approval by this diff (slice GG4b, a security part, never accepted narrower). It rests on the owner's
+rulings of 26 November 2026 (about 10:35 and 13:40 UTC): on the slice's draft every proposal (a), except how "may read" is decided and how
+a later re-marking is caught, which are (b); the board's ceiling is its own slice (GG4j). It amends decision 4. Points the rulings did not
+decide are marked **[for approval]** and gathered in item 11. ADR-152 decision 11 gains row X9 for the changes below the Glass.
+
+**The defect.** A drawn element's provenance names a cited document twice: `provenance.drawn.source_document`, and the `document_id` of
+the source the board writes. Every read path sends an element through `ontology.handling.withhold_sources`, which keeps the sources of an
+element whose datasets the reader's case reads, and a drawn item lives in the case's own layer. So the id reaches every member of the case
+who may see the item, whether or not they may read the document. (Where the sources are withheld, `source_document` is still sent.) The
+document is checked once, at drawing, with the author's principal. Nothing, in the code or this record, re-reads a drawn item when a
+document's marking later changes, so nothing recorded at drawing could be relied on afterwards.
+
+1. **Sent without the document.** A reader who may not read a cited document is sent the drawn node or link without it:
+   `drawn.source_document` is sent empty, and the source that named it is sent as `drawn:<item_id>`, the source of an item drawn from a
+   note alone. **Every document the board cited is covered, not only the one cited now:** an edit changes the assertion and leaves the
+   stored sources as they were, and a second write keeps every stored source (`merge_provenance`), so a source the board wrote may name a
+   document cited before an edit. Each such source is judged by its own document. Those withheld become **one** `drawn:<item_id>` source,
+   never repeated, so that the sources sent do not say how many documents were withheld, or that any was. The author keeps a document only
+   while the author may still read it.
+2. **"May read" is asked when the item is sent, of the person it is sent to.** The question is the one drawing asks, put by the graph
+   adapter's own access step: the id names an element that the reader's principal reads (its handling and the case's datasets) and that is a
+   `Document`. The principal is the reader's: never the author's, and never that of whoever put the item on a canvas. **Nothing about the
+   document is recorded on the drawn assertion, no answer is kept for a later one, and there is no reconciliation.** A document re-marked
+   after drawing, by any path, is therefore caught by the next send. What a client was already sent is not recalled, as for any element
+   re-marked while it is on a screen.
+3. **One batched read for an answer.** For a frame, a delta, a page of hits or any other answer, the documents its drawn elements cite are
+   read together, never one read for each element; an answer that cites none makes no read. Where one answer is put together from several
+   reads of the graph, each such read makes at most one **[for approval]**, so the number of reads never grows with the number of drawn
+   items. A cited id costs the same whether the document is read, hidden from the reader or gone. **A read that fails keeps nothing:** no
+   cited document is sent that the read did not find.
+4. **One place.** The rule is in `withhold_sources`, beside the board id's rule. It stays a pure function: the batch's result, the cited
+   documents this reader may read, is passed to it, and **given none it withholds every one**, so a caller that makes no read fails closed.
+   Through it go the graph adapter's serialisation (and so the board route, every node and link read, and search hits, which are read from
+   the graph) and the working set's projection (the snapshot, a delta, a replacement and a writer's own answer): no body the working set
+   stores is sent to a person except through it. The search index's builder has no reader: it passes none, and it indexes no provenance.
+5. **What is kept is kept whole.** What the working set holds, and what any write writes back, keeps the cited documents as stored; the
+   rule is applied at each send. A read that fills the working set, or whose result is written back (ADR-130 Amendment 2, "a read that is
+   written back"), is made with the cited documents left in. Otherwise one person's view would be stored as everyone's, and an author who
+   may no longer read a document would erase it by changing a label. Such a read's result goes to no one without the rule.
+6. **Why this meets rule 3.** The rule withholds fields of an element the reader may already see. It changes no traversal, degree, path,
+   count or histogram bucket, and which elements are sent does not depend on it. Whether the reader may read a document is decided by the
+   adapter's access step, as for every read. A reader's answers have the same shape whether a cited document is hidden from them or does
+   not exist.
+7. **A drawn item needs a source note (decision 4 amended).** Decision 4's "a source note (free text, a document the case already holds,
+   or both)" now reads: **a source note (free text), to which a document the case already holds may be added, never in its place.**
+   Declaring and linking refuse a body with no note, a blank one counting as none, by the body's own validation (422). **An edit that
+   would leave an item with no note is refused, 422 `no_source`, so an item drawn before this amendment gains its note at its next edit
+   [for approval].** Items already drawn with a document and no note stay as they are stored; nothing migrates them.
+8. **An item drawn before this amendment with a document and no note.** A reader who may not read its document is shown **"No source note
+   was given"**: the words every drawn item with an empty note is shown with, to every reader, byte for byte. No screen says that an empty
+   note means there is a document. **[for approval]** On the wire such a reader is sent no note and no document. So "a source note, a
+   document, or both; never neither" is no longer a rule of `DrawnAssertion` as it is read (`01_DOMAIN_MODEL.md` §2.2d and
+   `03_CONDUIT_MIDDLEWARE.md`'s boards are brought into line); the write routes hold item 7's rule instead. GG4b makes three small changes
+   to the front ends that this needs, and no other: the GOV.UK board page's reader stops refusing a drawn element with neither (as built it
+   would refuse that reader's whole board); the React board's inspector says "No source note was given" for an empty note, in place of
+   "None: a document of the case"; and the React drawing form asks for a note, with the document optional (its keyboard case in the same
+   change, rule 13). No page gains a source column.
+9. **What this still tells, stated so that it is approved knowingly.** Once item 7 holds, an item with an empty note can only be one drawn
+   before it, and such an item had to cite a document. A reader who knows that history can infer that **some** document was cited: not
+   which, nor anything about it. This is accepted in place of hiding the item in full. For an item drawn after this amendment, a reader
+   who may not read the document cannot tell a note with a document from a note alone.
+10. **Promotion (decision 8, "each item graded with a source note") [for approval].** As built, the check passes an item with a note or a
+    document, so on an item sent without its document its answer would differ by who asks. It asks for a note, whoever asks. An item drawn
+    before this amendment with no note is promoted once its author has added one.
+11. **For approval, in one place:** item 3's several reads for one answer; item 7's rule for edits; item 8's wire shape and three
+    front-end changes; item 10's promotion check.
+
+Not decided or changed here: the board's ceiling (GG4j); a drawn item re-marked other than by its edit route, as it bears on a waiting
+promotion (its own security row, before RL2); a picture's own handling (decision 7); the source references of elements that were not drawn;
+a source column on any page.
+
+Tests that would fail (rule 12; each written first and shown red on the code as it stood, with a fault planted at the real call site in the
+shape the code writes it, rule 15). The readers: **A** drew an item citing document D; **B** reads the case but may not read D; **C** reads
+the case and may read D. In `tests/ontology/test_drawn_source_document_withheld.py`, over `withhold_sources` and the boards service: B is
+sent a drawn node and a drawn link with D in neither place (red: today's code); C is sent D (red: the read skipped for C, the id kept from
+the author's check); A is sent D (red: the author's case inverted); an item drawn from a note alone is the same for all three (red: the
+note blanked too); an item with a document and no note is sent to B with an empty note and nothing else of the document; an item edited
+from one document to another sends B neither (red: only the current document blanked); and `sources_in_full()` keeps D (red: the scope
+ignored). Beside the existing tests, with the store each needs: the working set's snapshot on connect, a delta, a search hit and a link
+from the item to a loaded node send B no D (each red with the rule put in the board route alone); the graph adapter's conformance case for
+the node and the link, written before the change; the leak test over the same readers, B's answers the same shape whether or not D exists;
+a new item with a document and no note is refused (red: the old rule kept); the promotion check asks for a note whoever asks; and the
+working set with drawn items opens and takes deltas within its budget, with a number of document reads that does not grow with the number
+of items (red: one read for each element).
